WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
Almost everything you do as a SOC analyst starts with a log.

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:10.730
An alert fires — and behind that alert are log lines. Learn to read them, and the whole job opens up.

3
00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:15.380
A log is simply a record of something that happened on a system.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:23.970
A login is a log. A file opened is a log. A firewall allowing a connection is a log. One line, one event.

5
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:31.910
Logs pour in from everywhere. Endpoints — your EDR records processes, files, and network activity.

6
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.160
The network — firewalls, proxies, DNS, and IDS sensors.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.310
Identity — every logon, MFA prompt, and privilege change.

8
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.080
The cloud — API calls, sign-ins, and resource changes.

9
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.000
And applications — web servers, databases, and the business's own apps.

10
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.310
Here's how a log becomes an alert. First, the raw log is generated by a system.

11
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:59.850
It's shipped to the SIEM, which collects and normalizes millions of them a day.

12
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.600
A detection rule runs across them, looking for a suspicious pattern.

13
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.620
When the rule matches, it produces the alert that lands in your queue. The log was there all along.

14
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.810
To read any log, find five things.

15
00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:26.120
Who — the user or account. What — the action. When — the timestamp. Where — the host. And from where — the source IP. Those five turn a wall of text into a sentence you understand.

16
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.620
So never treat an alert as the whole story.

17
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:35.720
Behind it are the logs — and reading them, field by field, is where every investigation begins.
